          <h1>{title}</h1>
          <p class="pillar-intro">{intro}</p>
          <PageByline />
          <figure class="posting-feature">
            <img src="/images/posting/yoga-posting-sites.png" width="1200" height="630" alt={title} loading="eager" />
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Yoga publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on whether you teach, practise deeply, or just write about it.</p>
            <p>The big yoga media do not take open guest posts. The teacher resources take method and business content. The practice blogs are open. The brands run product-adjacent editorial. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Yoga &amp; wellness media</td><td>Yoga Journal, Yoga International, DoYou, MindBodyGreen, Gaia, Elephant Journal</td><td>Not open guest posts. A byline from an experienced teacher, yoga therapist or a fresh reported angle</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Teacher &amp; studio resources</td><td>Yoga Alliance, Yoga Basics, Yogapedia, Yoga Teacher Magazine, Yoga Teacher Resource</td><td>Contributions on sequencing, teaching method, business of teaching, anatomy</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Practice &amp; philosophy blogs</td><td>The Yoga Lunchbox, Yogic Way, Yogi Times, The Yogi Lifestyle, YogiClifestyle</td><td>Open to contributions; practice essays, philosophy, first-hand experience</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Yoga brands &amp; retreats</td><td>Alo Yoga, Yoga Outlet, YogaPaws, Yogi Surprise, Karma Surf Retreat</td><td>Product-adjacent education, retreat and travel content, teacher spotlights</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> yoga overlaps with health and mindfulness, so anything touching injury, therapy or medical claims needs real qualifications. A piece from a certified teacher or a yoga therapist carries weight a generic wellness post does not.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; dormant and shut-down blogs were replaced with active yoga publications. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {SITES.map((s) => (
                  <tr><td>{s.n}</td><td>{s.site}</td><td>{s.dr}</td><td>{s.da}</td><td>{s.pa}</td><td>{s.ur}</td><td>{s.traffic}</td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the teaching or the practice.</strong> "As a yoga therapist working with lower-back pain" or "what 10 years of Mysore practice taught me about consistency" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>State your credential.</strong> Certification, lineage, years teaching. Editors want to know who is giving the guidance.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your teaching resources" or "for the philosophy series" shows you read the site.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring your own detail.</strong> A sequence you actually teach, cues that work, a real student example (anonymised).</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> Media want a reported or personal piece. Teacher resources want a structured guide. Blogs want an honest essay.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any training or product</strong> you have a stake in.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it exists only to place a link, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a generic "5 poses for beginners" listicle with no teaching credential.</li>
              <li>Make injury, prenatal or therapy claims with no qualification attached.</li>
              <li>Send a lightly-reworded version of a post already on your own site.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links, or point exact-match anchors ("best yoga mat") at your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated "yoga tips" with no lived practice behind them.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on yoga media and teacher resources with real readers, written from actual practice or teaching, will do more for rankings and studio bookings than a long list of dormant yoga-blog links.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>{toc.map((item) => (<li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>))}</ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq"><Faq items={faqs} /></div>

      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="cta-panel" style="text-align: center; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
            <h2>Want us to run the outreach?</h2>
            <p>We build the shortlist by style and tier, pair physical pieces with a certified teacher or therapist, write the pitches and place them on real yoga publications. No link farms, no risk.</p>
            <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: center; margin-top: 0;">
              <a href="/services" class="button">View services</a>
              <a href={whatsappUrl('Hi SERP Mentor, I would like help with yoga and wellness guest posting and digital PR.')} class="button-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                <i class="fa-brands fa-whatsapp" aria-hidden="true"></i> WhatsApp
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
